<title>MEXICO: Mexico sets 1997 tariff-free corn import quotas.</title>
<headline>Mexico sets 1997 tariff-free corn import quotas.</headline>
<dateline>MEXICO CITY 1997-06-19</dateline>
<text>
<p>Mexico published Thursday the 1997 tariff-free corn import quotas for the United States and Canada under the North American Free Trade Agreement (NAFTA).</p>
<p>Via the government's Official Gazette, the Trade Ministry said it had allocated imports of 2,731,817 tonnes for U.S. corn and 1,093 tonnes of Canadian grain.</p>
<p>Individual corn millers and importers can request a portion of the country-wide quota as long as &quot;production processes use corn varieties not grown in Mexico and that they need to import corn to maintain their international competitivity.&quot;</p>
<p>The quotas expire on Dec. 31 1997.</p>
<p>((--Chris Aspin, Mexico City newsroom +525 7289530))</p>
